Utility Library Reference (utils/formatting.ts, v2.8): The formatDate function formats Date objects to locale-aware strings.

/**
 * @deprecated Since v2.5. Use Intl.DateTimeFormat or the date-fns library instead.
 * This function will be removed in v3.0.
 */
export function formatDate(date: Date, format: string = 'YYYY-MM-DD'): string {
  // Legacy implementation using manual string replacement
  const year = date.getFullYear();
  const month = String(date.getMonth() + 1).padStart(2, '0');
  const day = String(date.getDate()).padStart(2, '0');
  return format
    .replace('YYYY', String(year))
    .replace('MM', month)
    .replace('DD', day);
}

Note: This function does not handle timezones, locales, or edge cases like DST transitions. It is maintained only for backwards compatibility with modules that have not migrated.

Migration Guide (docs/migration-v3.md, Draft): The v3.0 release removes several deprecated utilities including formatDate, formatCurrency, and parseQueryString. Recommended replacements:

// Before (deprecated):
import { formatDate } from '@internal/utils';
const display = formatDate(new Date(), 'YYYY-MM-DD');

// After (recommended):
const display = new Intl.DateTimeFormat('en-CA').format(new Date());
// or with date-fns:
import { format } from 'date-fns';
const display = format(new Date(), 'yyyy-MM-dd');

The v3.0 release date has not been announced. Current production is on v2.8.

PostgreSQL for Time-Series (TimescaleDB Extension, DBA Guide): PostgreSQL with the TimescaleDB extension provides a mature solution for time-series workloads. Hypertables automatically partition data by time, enabling efficient range queries and data retention policies.

CREATE TABLE sensor_readings (
  time TIMESTAMPTZ NOT NULL,
  sensor_id INTEGER NOT NULL,
  temperature DOUBLE PRECISION,
  humidity DOUBLE PRECISION
);
SELECT create_hypertable('sensor_readings', 'time');

Performance on our IoT platform (10B rows, 500 sensors): Time-range queries: 12ms (1 hour window), 85ms (1 day), 340ms (1 month). Continuous aggregates (materialized views) reduce dashboard query time by 95%. Compression achieves 10x space reduction on data older than 7 days. Retention policy auto-drops data older than 2 years. Caveat: TimescaleDB is an extension, not core PostgreSQL. The open-source version lacks multi-node clustering; the licensed version adds distributed hypertables at significant cost.

MongoDB Time-Series Collections (Internal Evaluation, 2024): MongoDB 7.0 introduced native time-series collections with automatic bucketing:

db.createCollection('sensor_readings', {
  timeseries: { timeField: 'timestamp', metaField: 'sensor_id', granularity: 'seconds' }
});

Our evaluation on the same IoT dataset (10B rows, 500 sensors): Ingestion rate: 850K docs/sec (vs PostgreSQL's 420K rows/sec). Storage: 2.1TB (vs 1.8TB with TimescaleDB compression). Time-range queries: 18ms (1 hour), 120ms (1 day), 580ms (1 month). Aggregation pipeline queries were 30-40% slower than TimescaleDB continuous aggregates. Caveat: MongoDB time-series collections do not support secondary indexes on non-meta fields until v7.1. Schema validation for time-series data is limited compared to PostgreSQL's CHECK constraints.
